In case A, the forward reaction is favored. This means that the reactants are being consumed to form products at a faster rate than the reverse reaction. As a result of this disturbance, the system is no longer at equilibrium and will attempt to re-establish equilibrium by favoring the forward reaction. Consequently, the concentration of the reactants will decrease, and the concentration of the products will increase. This shift in concentrations reflects the system's response to the applied stress, and the chemical system will continue to shift in this direction until a new equilibrium is reached. The forward reaction is thus continuously favored until the system can no longer adjust, at which point a new balance is achieved.

In case B, the reverse reaction is favored. This means that the products are being consumed to form reactants at a faster rate than the forward reaction. The disturbance in equilibrium causes the system to respond by favoring the reverse reaction. This will result in an increase in the concentration of the reactants and a decrease in the concentration of the products. The system adjusts in a manner that opposes the stress, and the chemical system will continue to adjust until a new equilibrium is established. This ongoing adjustment continues until the concentrations stabilize and the equilibrium is reestablished.

To address the question, in case A, the concentration of the reactants would decrease compared to case B, where the concentration of the reactants would increase. This outcome results from the different reactions being favored in each scenario based on the applied stress, according to Le Châtelier's principle. The principle explains how systems naturally counteract changes, ensuring that the concentration of reactants and products shifts in a way that opposes the initial disturbance. Hence, in the context of this principle, the behavior of the system in both cases is a direct consequence of the specific stresses applied.
